6262556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186260469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262556 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186261864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262556 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262556 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262556 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186260028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262556 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa309f15-802f-59a4-8713-d59606b3ae60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262579 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262579 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262579 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262579 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74467f4e-ddb0-5476-baee-aa2c6186829d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262579 #edb/ref 1074 "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262644 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262644 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262644 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262644 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-3,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262644 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0aff07a4-c975-5d33-911a-8063644aae6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262644 #edb/ref 1074 "The point $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3-t}=2 \\\\ 1-2t=-3 \\\\ t+3=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(2).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-t}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-2}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\  & =-\\,i-2\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-3,5)$ is $-\\,i-2\\,j+k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262652 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262652 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair spinner has six sides labeled with the numbers $0,$ $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ $4,$ and $5.$ If the spinner is spun, what is the expected value of the outcome?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9bf879c-7d2c-5c41-9793-a5c97f1cf9de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262652 #edb/ref 1074 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the outcome of the spinner. Then the support is $S={0,1,2,3,4,5},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{6}:$\n\n$$\nf(0)=f(1)=f(2)=f(3)=f(4)=f(5)=\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(5)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{1}{6})+2(\\frac{1}{6})+3(\\frac{1}{6})+4(\\frac{1}{6})+5(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{2}{6}+\\frac{3}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+1+2+3+4+5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262685 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22f3b282-003f-570b-9433-d53609b5be7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262685 #edb/ref 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262694 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ y=5-4t \\\\ z=2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c62d423-8d11-5925-97f5-9b2d4f0ea158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262694 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ 5-4t \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}t \\\\ -4t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-2,5,\\frac{1}{7})$  and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262768 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262768 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-6t \\\\ y=1+3t \\\\ z=-5+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0277a440-31eb-5c11-98db-343c5921b893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262768 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-6t \\\\ 1+3t \\\\ -5+2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is  invertible\n- $Ax=0$ has a nonzero solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd31265e-20ac-5d02-8e25-ee3bd3fdfd6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262851 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262932 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262932 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262932 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262932 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x-8y+z=-1 \\\\ 9y=2 \\\\ 3x-y-8z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262932 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9041adde-3984-5731-b843-df3f6fc568b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262932 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5x-8y+z=-1 \\\\ 0x+9y+0z=2 \\\\ 3x-y-8z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & \\,\\,\\,-8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\,\\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,\\,-8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262950 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}p+3q=2 \\\\ 9p-4q=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "211f2486-98ca-57f9-afcd-978296d23dab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262950 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the system is already organized so that the variables are in the order $p,q$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient.   \n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262988 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{x-2}?$ \n\n- $[-3,-1]$\n- $[-1,1]$\n- $[1,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fc15eb5-6ce5-5be9-9bbc-fe517b5c7183" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262988 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-3,-1]$ is closed, but the function is not defined on the part of the interval where $x\\le -2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-1,1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,3]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262991 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)?$   \n\n- $[0,1]$\n- $[1,2]$\n- $(0,2)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "787a1177-1769-5956-b6cd-3399afd3dc9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262991 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous. \n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[0,1]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on it because it is not defined at $x=0.$  Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,2]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,2].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(0,2)$  is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262996 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262996 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262996 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262996 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ce5c5ca-b382-5ac8-8df3-b2e87f07de7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186262996 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since ${a}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the third row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since ${c}_{33}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -4 & 5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263001 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9f9477f-0f39-55d8-91e7-cff41b6de7c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263001 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186262271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the two planes $x-5y+2z=0$ and $3x-2y+7=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d75bf5e3-9255-56f7-a884-2df425d0b593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263002 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-5y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y+7=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\times ⟨3,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+6j+13k \\\\  & =⟨4,6,13⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263012 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263012 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263012 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "012cd8a6-b675-5154-9601-7a65d5de12d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263037 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263037 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263037 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04406210-94a5-519d-b563-c1787d94b573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263064 #edb/ref 1080 "0.0518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "073c5c5e-c861-57e9-945f-a83adfdf8a69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263071 #edb/ref 1080 "0.1084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263071 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263071 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07bf1876-cf7e-5384-b7fe-70631d2188d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263075 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-2,3,-1⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263075 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "083caf03-1501-5cba-8190-446a66b010a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263095 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263095 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263095 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09ffa2a2-ecec-56e6-820d-64d7134e3e5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263120 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨1,-\\frac{1}{3},2⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263120 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263120 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cbb9cc1-730a-501c-b1b3-c8376f37a85a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263128 #edb/ref 1080 "0.469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263128 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263128 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0dfe371c-c19a-5de8-9452-15e66ce169a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263142 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263142 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263142 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fbf0d98-bbd6-5024-9ccd-0001e83cbc2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263143 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921862